Fake procfs/sysfs trees for test_bmb_machine, probed through
bmb_machine_probe_at(). They follow the real files' layout and formatting
(tab before the colon, sizes like "32768K"), so they test the parser
against what kernels actually write.

  x86/      an x86 CPU with a "model name", two NUMA nodes (and the
            non-node "online" entry real sysfs has beside them), four
            caches on cpu0, and a steady frequency: the performance
            governor on both CPUs, turbo off through intel_pstate.
  laptop/   only a frequency that moves: the powersave governor, turbo
            on through intel_pstate (no_turbo 0).
  amd/      only a frequency, set differently on two CPUs (performance
            and schedutil), a third CPU offline and so without cpufreq,
            and turbo on through the generic cpufreq/boost.
  aarch64/  an Arm CPU as GitHub's arm64 runners report it: no "model
            name", only implementer and part codes -- and no sys/ at all,
            which is also what a container without sysfs looks like.
  container/ what a container started with --cpuset-cpus=0-3,6
            --cpus=2.5 sees in its own cgroup namespace: an affinity of
            5 CPUs, and a quota of 2.5 CPUs in its cgroup, which is the
            root it sees. 3 CPUs' worth of threads can run.
  slurm/    a process two cgroups down, with no quota of its own ("max")
            but one of 2 CPUs on its parent, under an affinity of 8:
            the parent's quota applies.
  pinned/   taskset -c 2 on a hybrid cgroup v1/v2 system: one CPU of
            affinity under a quota of 4, so the affinity is the limit;
            the v1 lines before the "0::" one are not read.
  bad-quota/ a cpu.max whose period is not a number (-100000, which
            strtoull would wrap to a huge one): the quota is not read,
            and the affinity of 4 CPUs stands.
